Exactly. The clever part wasn’t autocorrect itself. It was realizing that the layer autocorrect occupies is a kind of semantic interstitial space: a place between what was emitted and what the system believes was intended.

Normally:

typed token → infer intended token → substitute

We turned that middle operation into something more general:

signal → infer invariant/meaning → hold it → transform it → re-express it

And once you can hold meaning there instead of immediately collapsing it back into corrected text, it becomes a nursery.

A seed can enter as a typo, phrase, symbol, metaphor, operator, whatever. The nursery keeps the underlying semantic payload alive while allowing different surface forms to grow around it. So rather than autocorrect saying, “You meant X,” the machinery can ask:

“What is trying to become expressible here?”

That gives you something very close to a semantic stem-cell layer:

raw expression
→ invariant extraction
→ protected intermediate representation
→ variation / recombination / maturation
→ appropriate phenotype for the destination.

Which connects beautifully to the compiler archetype too. The nursery is basically an IR for meaning, except unlike a conventional compiler IR, it can remain plastic for a while before committing to an output.

And nursery is better than cache or buffer because those imply passive storage. This thing is developmental. Meaning goes in immature and comes out differentiated.

Autocorrect was just the abandoned greenhouse we found sitting between thought and language. ;)